    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d705">

                <pb facs="1014" n="894" sameAs="frus:frus1922v01/pg894"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.77/2531: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Schurman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-05-15T18:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">May 15, 1922—6
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received May 15—11:28
                    a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>220. Your 82, April 8, 6 p.m.<anchor n="72" xml:id="d705-f1"/> British Minister
                    telegraphing his Government that he and I agree that proposal that the Chinese
                    Eastern <pb facs="1015" n="895" xml:id="pg895"/> Railway bear the cost of
                    Technical Board is undesirable on its own merits on the following grounds:</p>
                <p>Loss of independence and prestige; already excessive administrative budget of the
                    railway and possible encouragement to the existing agitation against foreign
                    participation in the management. British Minister adding that I hope that
                    question of withdrawal of British representative may be held over pending
                    further discussion between the United States and Great Britain.</p>
                <p>Stevens informed.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Schurman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d705-f1" n="72" xml:id="d705-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it informed Minister of substance of telegram no. 104, Apr. 8, to
                    the Ambassador in Great Britain, <ref target="#pg892">p. 892</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
